team-1    | ╭────────────────── Spec: Binary Search Tree module (bst.py) ──────────────────╮
team-1    | │ A Python module `bst.py` at the workspace root exposing class                │
team-1    | │ `BinarySearchTree` with `insert(value) -> None`, `search(value) -> bool` and │
team-1    | │ `inorder() -> list` (ascending order). Duplicate inserts are ignored. Values │
team-1    | │ are assumed mutually comparable. Only the standard library and pytest are    │
team-1    | │ used. Tests live in `tests/test_bst.py` and exercise every behaviour and     │
team-1    | │ code branch (both left and right descent, empty tree, duplicates, errors).   │
team-1    | │ acceptance criteria:                                                         │
team-1    | │   AC1: `bst.py` at the workspace root can be imported (`from bst import      │
team-1    | │ BinarySearchTree`) and `BinarySearchTree()` can be constructed with no       │
team-1    | │ arguments.                                                                   │
team-1    | │   AC2: On a new empty tree, `inorder()` returns `[]` and `search(x)` returns │
team-1    | │ `False` for any x.                                                           │
team-1    | │   AC3: `insert(value)` returns `None` (for both new and duplicate values).   │
team-1    | │   AC4: After inserting values, `search(v)` returns `True` for every inserted │
team-1    | │ value and `False` for values not inserted (including values smaller than the │
team-1    | │ minimum, larger than the maximum, and between existing values). The return   │
team-1    | │ value is a real `bool`.                                                      │
team-1    | │   AC5: `inorder()` returns all inserted values in ascending order regardless │
team-1    | │ of insertion order (ascending, descending, and shuffled insertion all give   │
team-1    | │ the same sorted result).                                                     │
team-1    | │   AC6: Duplicate inserts are ignored: inserting the same value one or more   │
team-1    | │ additional times leaves it appearing exactly once in `inorder()`, and        │
team-1    | │ `search` still returns `True` for it.                                        │
team-1    | │   AC7: `inorder()` returns a new list each call; mutating the returned list  │
team-1    | │ does not change the tree or later `inorder()` results.                       │
team-1    | │   AC8: Works with any mutually comparable values: ints (including negatives  │
team-1    | │ and zero), floats, and strings each work in their own tree; mixed int and    │
team-1    | │ float values are ordered correctly.                                          │
team-1    | │   AC9: Inserting or searching a value that cannot be compared with the       │
team-1    | │ values already in a non-empty tree (e.g. a str into an int tree) raises      │
team-1    | │ `TypeError`, and a failed insert leaves the tree contents unchanged.         │
team-1    | │   AC10: Degenerate trees do not cause recursion errors: inserting 5000       │
team-1    | │ values in strictly ascending order, then calling `insert`, `search` and      │
team-1    | │ `inorder()`, completes correctly with no `RecursionError` (implementation    │
team-1    | │ must be iterative, not recursive).                                           │
team-1    | │   AC11: The test suite `tests/test_bst.py` exists, passes under `pytest`,    │
team-1    | │ and contains tests covering every acceptance criterion above, including      │
team-1    | │ empty tree, single element, left-only and right-only descent, and both       │
team-1    | │ branches of duplicate handling.                                              │
