team-1    |     edge_case bst.py:BinarySearchTree.insert: Values are only assumed mutually comparable; with mixed or non-total-order types (e.g. None, NaN floats) the comparisons `value < current.value` can raise TypeError midway through descent, after no mutation has happened, but NaN silently fails both < checks and falls into the duplicate branch, so NaN is never inserted yet reports as a no-op; no test covers this.
team-1    |     edge_case tests/test_bst.py: Tests only cover a single node and duplicate at root. Task requires the left and right descent branches and the iterative inorder stack to be exercised; currently the descent branches of insert/search and the stack loop of inorder are never hit with more than one level.
team-1    |     edge_case bst.py:BinarySearchTree.search: Search with a value of an incomparable type on a non-empty tree raises TypeError, but on an empty tree returns False silently; behaviour is inconsistent and untested.
team-1    |     edge_case bst.py:BinarySearchTree.inorder: State persistence: inorder returns a fresh list each call, but the result is built from node values so mutating the returned list does not affect the tree; no test verifies this, and no test verifies that repeated calls are independent.
team-1    |     edge_case bst.py:_Node / BinarySearchTree.__init__: No public way to check emptiness besides inorder; no test that a fresh instance does not share root state with another instance (class-level mutable state would break this), and no test that two trees are independent.
