team-1    |     bug tests/test_bst.py: test_t2_ascending_descending_shuffled_identical / shuffled case: Shuffled order is generated with random.Random(12345) and compared to a fixed expected list; this is deterministic so it is fine, but it only exercises one permutation. A failure in an unbalanced-shape path (e.g. a bug only hit when inserting a middle value into a deep left subtree) could be missed.
team-1    |     edge_case tests/test_bst.py: test_t2_duplicate_does_not_add_nodes: The test only checks inorder output, which would look identical if duplicates were stored as extra nodes that inorder dedups. It does not verify that no nodes were added, which is what the acceptance criterion about duplicates not adding nodes requires.
team-1    |     edge_case tests/test_bst.py: test_t2_inorder_list_mutation_does_not_affect_later_calls: Mutating the returned list is tested, but not mutating a list obtained from an empty tree or a single-element tree, where an implementation returning a shared module-level or cached default list would slip through.
team-1    |     edge_case bst.py: inorder() (iterative implementation): Iterative traversal with an explicit stack is easy to get wrong when a node has only a right child or only a left child at the boundary; the existing deeper-tree test covers only mixed shapes. Chain-shaped trees (all-left and all-right) are not tested for inorder.
team-1    |     bug tests/test_bst.py: test_t2_ascending_insertion_gives_sorted_output / test_t2_descending_insertion_gives_sorted_output: Both use range(20), producing a fully degenerate tree, so the tests pass whether or not the tree is balanced; they don't check that insert with duplicates handled at each level of a skewed chain works.
team-1    |     edge_case bst.py: insert() duplicate path with mixed types: Duplicate detection relies on comparisons; inserting a value equal across types (e.g. 1 and 1.0, or True and 1) will be treated as duplicate and ignored, which may be surprising and is not tested or documented.
team-1    |     edge_case tests/test_bst.py: test_t2_repeated_duplicates_collapse: Only verifies the output list; it does not check that search still works for the collapsed values or that insertion after duplicates continues to place new values correctly.
